…`) become exclusions, and directories are still traversed when they could contain matches. I used only dependencies the project already declares (`anymatch`, `glob-parent`, `normalize-path`) plus Node's `fs`.\n\n**Checks:** The test suite isn't in the workspace, so I wrote scratch scripts in a temp directory and deleted them afterwards. They confirmed:\n- a change to a matching file in a subdirectory emits `change`;\n- a change to a non-matching `.txt` file emits nothing;\n- files under a negated glob (`!src/skip/**`) are excluded;\n- a newly created matching file emits `add`, and a deleted one emits `unlink`;\n- `add()` picks up new globs, and plain (non-glob) paths work.\n\n**Choices and limitations:**\n- The user's `ignored` option is passed through `anymatch`, so string entries keep glob semantics as in 3.x. Chokidar 4 would have treated them as exact paths.\n- `unwatch()` removes the glob and stops watching its base directory. However, chokidar 4 still reports changes to files it was already tracking after an `unwatch` on their directory (I reproduced this with plain chokidar, without gulp-watch). Those events then trigger gulp-watch's existing \"unexpected path\" log message. I left that alone rather than add filtering that would change logging behaviour.\n- I did not run the project's own test suite, because it isn't in this workspace.","ttft_ms":3193,"type":"result","duration_ms":156105,"uuid":"ec59a46b-8d89-4f98-b0ed-c1bbdd9b38ea","begun_message_ids":["msg_011ChMSsGKvo8bx9k3wu92bi","msg_011ChMSsZCw5Pzi6AAEfsfqC","msg_011ChMSsd1PofGZA2DYaMazz","msg_011ChMSsv34whjmfE86rVJ2b","msg_011ChMSvQ6LzY2vXSj6nhdfC","msg_011ChMSvVjB1zXfF5WoSTrVA","msg_011ChMSxPd7uroq3xkDS95E3","msg_011ChMSyVZLmXJMYNLDDg1Vn","msg_011ChMSykeBnaWNpMFqiDsoX","msg_011ChMSyxusxrU7sJZNY3PXM","msg_011ChMSzMG3Fy3igxkuzcdDr","msg_011ChMSzaT45RYNFpUSC6aUT","msg_011ChMSzgWvR75qFaDUfx64t","msg_011ChMSzzC4FU7Uom24kqQ6p","msg_011ChMT1NeAxYAdb6cRwfPaE","msg_011ChMT1aL8ujCMm2169Ucm9","msg_011ChMT29dNFe